## Relicensing rationale

CC-BY-4.0 is a license intended for creative works rather than software, and
Creative Commons itself recommends against its use for code (see
https://creativecommons.org/faq/#can-i-apply-a-creative-commons-license-to-software).
This causes practical and legal ambiguity for downstream consumers.

The code in `packages/`, `apps/`, `examples/`, `benchmarks/`, `docs/`,
`scripts/`, and `infra/` of this monorepo is either:

- newly authored work by the maintainers of this repository, or
- a derivative work created from upstream OmniParser that has been
  substantially rewritten (refactored module structure, type annotations,
  error handling, logging, packaging, dependency injection of OCR/detection
  backends, removal of dead code, and documentation).

Per the attribution requirement of CC-BY-4.0 (Section 3.a.1), the upstream
authors are credited below. Per Section 3.a.4, this NOTICE indicates that
the original work has been modified.

The reorganized code in this repository is licensed under the MIT License
(see `LICENSE`).

